<div class="tab-guide-body" data-guide-body="guide_body_track_record" hidden data-i18n-html="guide_body_track_record">
  <p>Both numbers are <strong>excess return over ACWI</strong>, in percentage
  points, for the horizon selected above — the strategy's return minus the
  benchmark's over the same window.</p>
  <p>They are <strong>point-in-time</strong>: the backtest curve reflects the
  themes the strategy actually held at each historical rebalance, including
  the hysteresis band. It is not today's picks measured backwards, which
  would credit the strategy with picks it had not made.</p>
  <h3>Why two windows</h3>
  <p>One month of a five-holding thematic strategy is mostly noise. The same
  strategy on the same day can read <strong>+1.2pp over one month and
  +32.2pp over twelve</strong> — the window picks the story. Both are shown
  so neither is read alone.</p>
  <p>Returns are net of the round-trip trading cost assumption, the same
  caveat the Backtest tab carries. Figures come from the last
  <code>backtest.py</code> run; when that becomes old, the chips show the
  date they are current as of.</p>
</div>
